  "seven_contiguous_BODY_regions": [
    {
      "title": "Fix the actual source objects and stopped event times",
      "start": 74,
      "end": 116,
      "sha256": "8472ac978ac71c8dc9b958c18b6fc25ac10821d1ab0fbe7c5dc99eb5dbcd72aa",
      "formula": "H(z)=\\tfrac12(\\eta^{-1}\\|x-c\\|^2+\\|p\\|^2),\\quad C=\\sqrt\\eta\\,\\beta\\sqrt{2H(z_0)}(\\sqrt{2\\eta H(z_0)}+\\|c-x_{\\rm ref}\\|).",
      "text": "Use the literal harmonic flow, zero-safe reflection, integrated rate, first hitting time and stopped recursion from the actual finite-prefix theorem. Fix the canonical Exp1 product and its total nonnegative thresholds. The energy H and finite cap C are internal formulas: H(z)=(η⁻¹‖x−c‖²+‖p‖²)/2 and C=√η β√(2H(z0))(√(2ηH(z0))+‖c−xRef‖). They are proved recurrence invariants and bounds, not theorem assumptions."
    },
    {
      "title": "Join the two actual compiled parents",
      "start": 117,
      "end": 123,
      "sha256": "85cd1db02123c7b64221467e5741091ed9422ee2bbcdd8005b452db8f6b8c0c2",
      "formula": "C\\ge0,\\quad C>0\\Longrightarrow T_n+\\varepsilon_n/C\\le T_{n+1},\\quad C=0,\\ \\varepsilon_n>0\\Longrightarrow\\text{next active record stops}.",
      "text": "Apply the finite-recursion theorem to the six analytic source hypotheses. It supplies initialization, monotone times, absorbing stopping, C≥0, the positive-cap increment and zero-cap stopping. Apply the concrete product theorem to obtain a common positive-threshold event and divergent partial sums, with no supplied stochastic witness."
    },
    {
      "title": "Intersect the two good-input events",
      "start": 124,
      "end": 128,
      "sha256": "1ca606f5abab82521b192f3db9ed74836da823a941f3a6827d3e9b76fe22a8a9",
      "formula": "P\\text{-a.s.}:\\quad\\forall k,\\ \\varepsilon_k>0,\\qquad S_n:=\\sum_{k<n}\\varepsilon_k\\longrightarrow+\\infty.",
      "text": "For arbitrary fixed y,xRef,z0, intersect the almost-sure all-coordinate positivity and divergent-sum events. Positivity makes every clamped ε_k strictly positive. Extract the cap laws for this actual sample; these facts remain internal to the proof."
    },
    {
      "title": "Handle zero cap by absorbing stopping",
      "start": 129,
      "end": 142,
      "sha256": "78fc652000fb8b533f0e10be2428989ca6dccfb209174ad0d3827211d8604671",
      "formula": "C=0\\Longrightarrow\\operatorname{record}_1=\\mathrm{stopped}\\Longrightarrow\\forall n\\ge1,\\ T_n=\\infty>t.",
      "text": "Split on C=0. The initial record is active and ε0>0, so its first update stops. Absorption proves T_(1+k)=∞ for every k. Every finite horizon is therefore exceeded at all indices n≥1. This includes zero-energy and dimension-zero cases."
    },
    {
      "title": "Sum the actual positive-cap increments",
      "start": 143,
      "end": 166,
      "sha256": "859040dda9835528764f052ecc6bac7d1e96ecdbf07baf84cbb2cda62efe8cbc",
      "formula": "C>0\\Longrightarrow\\forall n,\\quad \\frac{S_n}{C}\\le T_n.",
      "text": "In the other branch C≥0 and C≠0 give C>0. Let cap=toNNReal(C), whose real coercion is C. Starting from S0=T0=0, apply the actual extended-valued increment inequality and induction: S_(n+1)/C=S_n/C+ε_n/C≤T_n+ε_n/C≤T_(n+1). The same inequality remains valid after stopping since times are top."
    },
    {
      "title": "Use divergent inputs to escape every finite horizon",
      "start": 167,
      "end": 177,
      "sha256": "1780ce9b2561348136790ae30f0decf7e7455c78e0b5d02a82710468c7d030d4",
      "formula": "S_n>tC\\Longrightarrow t<S_n/C\\le T_n.",
      "text": "For any finite t, divergence gives S_n>tC eventually. Because C>0 this implies S_n/C>t. Combine this strict finite inequality, lifted into WithTop NNReal, with the prefix lower bound. This proves finite-horizon escape without requiring eventual equality to infinity."
    },
    {
      "title": "Turn escape into a finite index set",
      "start": 178,
      "end": 184,
      "sha256": "093d05764bb1629ea7f6d2ef93966edf23bc72b4c274a295976b6b782d60c6af",
      "formula": "\\{n:T_n\\le t\\}\\subseteq\\{0,\\ldots,N-1\\}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\#\\{n:T_n\\le t\\}<\\infty.",
      "text": "For each t, choose N such that n≥N implies t<T_n. Any n with T_n≤t must satisfy n<N. The sublevel index set is therefore contained in the finite natural interval [0,N); index0 is retained. Assemble both almost-sure conclusions."
